(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-pdm-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A dipstick supports a urinary tract infection. What identifies the organism and what will treat it?","choices":["Urine culture and sensitivity","Gram stain of the urine alone","A repeat dipstick after treatment","Blood cultures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — culture names the organism and sensitivity picks the agent. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"On the Hounsfield scale, where water is zero, which tissue sits at roughly minus forty to minus one hundred and twenty?","choices":["Fat, which is why it reads darker than soft tissue but lighter than air","Soft tissue, which is why it reads darker than fat but lighter than water","Bone, which is why it reads brighter than soft tissue but darker than metal","Air, which is why it reads darker than every other tissue on the scan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — fat is negative, but nowhere near as negative as air. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Using the standard formula, what is the normal range for the anion gap?","choices":["12 to 20 milliequivalents per litre","8 to 12 milliequivalents per litre","10 to 14 milliequivalents per litre","4 to 8 milliequivalents per litre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the normal range. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Beyond the heart, what else is better seen on the posterior-anterior chest view?","choices":["Maximum visualisation of the lung, better visualisation of the apices, and well visualised posterior ribs","Maximum visualisation of the mediastinum, better visualisation of the hila, and well visualised anterior ribs","Maximum visualisation of the diaphragm, better visualisation of free air, and well visualised gastric bubble","Maximum visualisation of the pleura, better visualisation of layering fluid, and well visualised costophrenic angles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — all three are listed alongside the cardiac and dose advantages. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is a schistocyte, and what else is it called?","choices":["A perfectly round red cell, also called a spherocyte or a microspherocyte","A crescent-shaped red cell, also called a drepanocyte","A teardrop-shaped red cell, also called a dacrocyte","A fragmented red cell, also called a helmet cell or a horn cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fragments of widely varying shapes and sizes. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What should be looked at in addition to the four evaluation steps?","choices":["A bone marrow biopsy in every case","Hemoglobin electrophoresis in every case","A direct antiglobulin test in every single case","The peripheral blood smear, if one can be obtained"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the peripheral blood smear. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"After three days of intractable vomiting, what does the panel show?","choices":["Low sodium, raised potassium, raised chloride, and a low bicarbonate with acidaemia","Raised sodium, raised potassium, and a normal bicarbonate","Normal electrolytes with an isolated raised anion gap","Low sodium, low potassium, low chloride, and a raised bicarbonate with alkalaemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is the pattern. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which causes of a raised hematocrit do not also raise the hemoglobin?","choices":["Chronic kidney disease and cancer","Trauma and acute hemorrhage","Iron deficiency and thalassemia","Smoking and hypoventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the hematocrit slide adds these two alongside polycythemia vera and dehydration. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How should the swab be applied when obtaining a throat culture?","choices":["Swab the anterior tongue and the inner cheeks","Swab a single tonsil to minimise the gag reflex","Swab the soft palate and uvula only, deliberately avoiding the tonsils so that the gag reflex is not triggered","Rotate it firmly and gently over the posterior throat, both tonsils, and any areas of inflammation, exudation or ulceration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the tongue and lips must not be touched. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient with ketonuria has a normal blood glucose and reports not eating for two days. What explains the ketones?","choices":["Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus","A urinary tract infection","Starvation","Overhydration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fasting drives fatty acid metabolism just as insulin deficiency does. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does the reticulocyte count tell you, and when is it most helpful?","choices":["Whether iron stores are adequate, and it is most helpful when ferritin is normal","Whether hemolysis is immune or mechanical, and it is most helpful when the smear is normal","Whether the bone marrow is functioning appropriately, and it is most helpful when very elevated or very decreased","Whether the bone marrow is functioning appropriately, and it is most helpful when in the middle of its range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the middle of the range is the least informative part. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which term describes tissue that appears white because it absorbs more of the beam?","choices":["Radiopaque, also called hyperdense or radiodense","Radiolucent, also called hypodense","Isodense, matching the density of the surrounding tissue","Attenuated, meaning the beam has been scattered away"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — less passage of x-ray photons produces the whiter appearance. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Besides the liver, in which four tissues is aspartate aminotransferase found?","choices":["Bone, placenta, intestine, and kidney","Spleen, pancreas, lung, and thyroid","Red blood cells, platelets, bone marrow, and lymph nodes","Cardiac muscle, skeletal muscle, kidney, and brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those four. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is a teardrop cell also called, where does it form, and what is it associated with?","choices":["A dacrocyte, formed within bone marrow infiltrated by scar tissue or cancerous cells, associated with bone marrow disease","A drepanocyte, formed within the circulation under low oxygen tension, associated with sickle cell anemia","A codocyte, formed from redundant membrane, associated with liver disease","An echinocyte, formed from membrane changes, associated with renal disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the shape reflects the marrow the cell had to squeeze out of. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"In the normocytic branch of the algorithm, which test is obtained next?","choices":["Iron studies","A peripheral smear looking for megaloblastic change","Hemoglobin analysis","The reticulocyte count"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a normal mean corpuscular volume sends you to the reticulocyte count. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What distinguishes a screening test from a diagnostic test?","choices":["Screening looks for evidence of disease in an asymptomatic person; a diagnostic test looks for the reason for symptoms","Screening is always more invasive than diagnostic testing","Screening provides the definitive diagnosis","Screening is performed only after a diagnostic test has returned an abnormal result that requires further explanation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the presence or absence of symptoms is the discriminator. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which hormone lowers blood glucose, and which raise it?","choices":["Insulin lowers it; antidiuretic hormone raises it","Insulin lowers it; glucagon and the counter-regulatory hormones raise it","Glucagon lowers it; insulin and cortisol raise it","Cortisol lowers it; insulin and adrenaline together raise it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — insulin lowers it; glucagon and the counter-regulatory hormones raise it. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the purpose of tonometry?","choices":["To measure intraocular pressure and evaluate for glaucoma","To measure corneal thickness and evaluate for keratoconus","To assess the visual pathway and evaluate for field loss","To assess corneal integrity and evaluate for abrasion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both halves of the stated purpose. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"When carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions rise, bicarbonate moves out of the cell. What does chloride do?","choices":["It binds to haemoglobin and is carried to the lungs for excretion","It shifts back into the cell to preserve electrical neutrality","It shifts out of the cell alongside bicarbonate","It is excreted by the kidney within minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the reciprocal shift. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What margin does the melanoma rule specify for the diagnostic biopsy?","choices":["A narrow excisional biopsy with margins of one to three millimetres","A wide excisional biopsy with margins of one to two centimetres","A narrow excisional biopsy with margins of one to three centimetres","An excisional biopsy with no margin, cutting at the visible lesion edge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — narrow margins, because this biopsy is for diagnosis rather than cure. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How does handheld point-of-care equipment differ from a benchtop device?","choices":["Handheld equipment is easy to carry, involves one to two steps, and usually gives one or few data points; benchtop devices are stationary, multi-step, and give multiple data points","Handheld equipment requires no reagents while benchtop devices do","Handheld equipment is quantitative while benchtop devices are qualitative","Handheld equipment requires a laboratory certificate and trained operators, whereas benchtop devices may be run by anyone on site without additional certification or documented competency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — portability, complexity and data yield all differ. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the difference between a complete blood count with and without differential?","choices":["The version with differential adds the breakdown of white cell types to everything the version without it contains","The version with differential adds platelet indices to everything the version without it contains","The version without differential omits the red cell indices","The version without differential omits the total white cell count"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one is a strict superset of the other. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the urine pH reflect?","choices":["Tubular handling of hydrogen ions","The rate at which the glomerulus filters plasma","The concentration of protein crossing the glomerulus","The total volume of urine produced each day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it reports tubular handling of hydrogen ions. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which agency categorizes tests based on complexity under the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments?","choices":["The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","The Joint Commission","The Food and Drug Administration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it also reviews waiver applications and develops categorization guidance. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What may be used to help a patient who cannot produce a sputum specimen?","choices":["Delaying collection until the following morning","Aerosols such as sterile water, saline or albuterol","Chest percussion alone, without any aerosol","A prolonged breath hold before coughing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — these help loosen and induce secretions. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What structural change occurs in the glaucomatous optic disc?","choices":["Lamina cribrosa collapse","Thickening of the retinal nerve fibre layer","Detachment of the neurosensory retina","Swelling of the optic nerve head"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the schematic panel shows collapse of the lamina cribrosa. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How does a computed tomography scanner acquire its data?","choices":["High-frequency sound waves released from a transducer bounce off tissue and return","A varying magnetic field aligns hydrogen atoms which then emit radio waves","Powerful x-ray beams pass through the patient via a rotating fan beam, measuring transmission at thousands of data points","A gamma camera mounted on a rotating gantry detects the photons that are emitted from an injected isotope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a computer then processes the data through algorithms into diagnostic images. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does specific gravity measure?","choices":["The osmotic pressure the urine exerts","The number of particles per litre of urine","Solute weight against an equal volume of water","The volume of urine produced per hour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is a ratio of weights. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How is gross hematuria distinguished from microscopic hematuria?","choices":["Gross is painless; microscopic is painful","Gross means more than three red cells; microscopic means fewer","Gross is visible to the naked eye; microscopic needs analysis to detect","Gross comes from the kidney; microscopic comes from the bladder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — visibility is the distinction. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does a urinalysis examine?","choices":["Only the microscopic sediment, after centrifugation","Only the chemical contents, read from a reagent strip","The physical, chemical, and microscopic contents of urine","The bacterial species present and their antibiotic sensitivities"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — all three together make up the test. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the five strengths of magnetic resonance imaging?","choices":["Soft-tissue contrast, speed, bone erosion, gas and oedema","No ionizing radiation, cystic versus solid character, vascularity, size and the depth of a neck mass on ultrasound","Superior soft-tissue contrast, no ionizing radiation, intracranial or orbital extension, perineural spread, skull base and tumours","Speed, wide availability, bone detail, foreign bodies and acute trauma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the magnetic resonance list as given. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which of these can produce glucosuria even when the serum glucose is within normal limits?","choices":["Renal disease that impairs tubular reabsorption","A diet high in meat","Dehydration","A urinary tract infection with Escherichia coli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the leak is tubular rather than a high blood level. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What causes INCREASED neutrophils?","choices":["Parasitic infection, allergic reaction, cancer, and pregnancy","Bacterial infection, myocardial infarction, burns, steroids, rheumatoid arthritis, and physiologic causes such as pregnancy, labor and surgery","Bacterial infection, radiation exposure, severe burns, benzene and other toxic chemicals, and an overwhelming systemic infection","Viral infection, folate deficiency, bone marrow damage, and steroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the full list, including the physiologic causes. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the normal neutrophil percentage, and what is the neutrophil's rank among white cells?","choices":["Twenty-four to forty-four per cent, and the most abundant white blood cell","Fifty-four to sixty-two per cent, and the second most abundant white blood cell","Three to seven per cent, and the largest white blood cell","Fifty-four to sixty-two per cent, and the most abundant white blood cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both the range and the rank, and the reference table agrees with this figure. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does serum osmolality allow you to distinguish?","choices":["Acute from chronic kidney disease","True hypotonic hyponatraemia from the pseudo- and hypertonic forms","Prerenal failure from intrinsic renal failure","Raised-gap from normal-gap acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — osmolality makes that separation. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why are three separate random stool specimens recommended for ova and parasite testing?","choices":["Because the first specimen is always discarded as contaminated","Because the laboratory requires a minimum total volume spread across three containers","Because of the life cycle of parasites, which increases the likelihood of detection","Because each specimen tests for a different class of organism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — organisms are shed intermittently, so a single specimen can miss them. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which tracer does positron emission tomography typically use, and why that one?","choices":["Technetium-99, a metastable isotope, because tumours retain it far longer than normal tissue","Iodixanol, an iso-osmolal agent, because tumours take up iodine more avidly than muscle","Gadolinium, a paramagnetic metal, because tumours disrupt the local magnetic environment","Fluorodeoxyglucose-18, a radioactive glucose, because tumours have a higher metabolic rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the scan asks which tissues are consuming more glucose. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Who discovered x-rays, and what recognition followed?","choices":["Godfrey Hounsfield, in Britain in 1972, awarded the Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1979","Allan Cormack, in South Africa in 1963, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics","Marie Curie, in France in 1898, awarded the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1903","Wilhelm Roentgen, in Germany in 1895, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the technique is still called roentgenography after him. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"When does the pretest phase begin and end?","choices":["It begins when the order is placed and ends when the specimen reaches the laboratory","It begins at the patient's arrival and ends when consent is signed","It begins when the specimen is collected and ends when it is analysed","It begins with patient preparation and extends until the diagnostic test begins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — preparation through to the moment the test starts. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which Breslow depth corresponds to T2?","choices":["More than two millimetres and up to four millimetres","More than four millimetres","More than one millimetre and up to two millimetres","One millimetre or less"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — T2 spans the interval above one and up to two millimetres. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which three standard planes are used in cross-sectional imaging, and how does each divide the body?","choices":["Axial into upper and lower, coronal into right and left, sagittal into anterior and posterior","Axial into upper and lower, coronal into anterior and posterior, sagittal into right and left","Axial into anterior and posterior, coronal into right and left, sagittal into upper and lower","Axial into right and left, coronal into upper and lower, sagittal into anterior and posterior"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — axial is also called transverse and is the commonest of the three. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What are the four causes of a decreased hematocrit?","choices":["Trauma, iron deficiency, polycythemia vera and acute hemorrhage","Anemias, acute or chronic blood loss, hemolysis and dilution","Polycythemia vera, smoking, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and dehydration","Bone marrow disorders, cancer, chronic kidney disease and dehydration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hemolysis is on the hematocrit list specifically. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Serum sodium and total-body sodium reflect different things. Which pairing is correct?","choices":["Serum sodium reflects total-body sodium; water balance governs extracellular volume","Both serum sodium and total-body sodium reflect water balance","Serum sodium reflects extracellular volume; water balance governs total-body sodium","Serum sodium reflects water balance; total-body sodium governs extracellular volume"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — serum sodium reflects water balance; total-body sodium governs extracellular volume. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"For what is computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging reserved in soft tissue infection?","choices":["Cellulitis that has failed forty-eight hours of oral antibiotics","Any infection in an immunocompromised patient","Deep-space infection, necrotizing infection, foreign body and gas","Any abscess large enough to require drainage in theatre"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the four reasons listed to escalate beyond ultrasound. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is the swab handled in the Levine method?","choices":["Pressed onto the wound edge and held still for five seconds","Rotated for five seconds over one to two centimetres of clean wound tissue, with enough pressure to express fluid","Rolled once across the entire width of the wound bed from edge to edge without applying pressure","Rotated for thirty seconds over the whole wound bed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — area, duration and pressure, all three as specified. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What kind of specimen does reagent strip testing require?","choices":["Any specimen collected within the past day","A specimen collected after a preservative is added","A fresh specimen in a sterile container","A first-morning specimen only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both the freshness and the sterile container matter. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which vascular magnetic resonance studies are performed without contrast?","choices":["Coronary angiography and retrograde pyelography","Arthrography and intrathecal myelography","Magnetic resonance angiography and magnetic resonance venography","Computed tomography angiography and computed tomography venography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both are done without a contrast agent. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A patient has heavy protein on the dipstick and foamy urine. Which finding on inspection matched the pad?","choices":["The colour","The foam","The odour","The transparency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — foaming is associated with protein. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the three indications for tympanometry?","choices":["Persistent otitis media with effusion, hearing loss, and retrocochlear pathology","Any ear pain, any ear discharge, and any hearing complaint","Suspected effusion, otitis media with effusion, and eustachian tube dysfunction","Suspected effusion, asymmetric hearing loss, and tinnitus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the three indications as given. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What counts as a fail on primary-care conventional audiometry?","choices":["Any threshold above zero decibels hearing level","More than twenty decibels hearing level at one or more frequencies","More than ten decibels hearing level at one or more frequencies","More than forty decibels hearing level at two or more frequencies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the threshold for a fail. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient asks why you are ordering a laboratory test when you already suspect the diagnosis. Which statement best describes the role of laboratory testing?","choices":["It is ordered chiefly to satisfy documentation requirements","It is a tool used alongside a thorough history and physical examination to add information","It replaces the history and physical examination once the results have been reported","It is primarily therapeutic rather than diagnostic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostics supplement the history and examination rather than replacing them. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Under the acute illness indication, which two situations call for a chemistry panel?","choices":["Headache and a suspected migraine","Dehydration and a suspected acid-base disorder","Fever and a suspected bloodstream infection","Chest trauma and a suspected rib fracture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those two go together under acute illness. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are the three things contrast computed tomography of the neck evaluates in deep neck infection?","choices":["A drainable abscess, airway compromise, and spread towards the mediastinum","Cystic versus solid character, size, and vascularity","Perineural spread, skull base involvement, and intracranial extension","Bone erosion, gas, and foreign body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the three questions this study is asked. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where does calcium sit among the panel components?","choices":["On its own, as the mineral","With sodium and potassium as an electrolyte","With the liver and protein markers","With blood urea nitrogen as a kidney marker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — calcium stands alone as the mineral. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Besides renal excretion, what is the other mechanism regulating potassium?","choices":["Loss through sweat and insensible routes","Transcellular shifts driven by insulin, acid-base status, and catecholamines","Storage in, and release from, the glycogen held in skeletal muscle","Binding to and release from plasma albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those three drive transcellular shift. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What does the integration component of the posttest phase refer to?","choices":["Entering results into the electronic health record","Reconciling the result against the pretest probability","Diagnosis, subsequent acceptance, healing, and health-promoting behavior","Combining results from several laboratories into a single unified record"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it includes patient education for the diagnosis and treatment plan. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A dipstick shows heavy proteinuria. Which test quantifies it properly?","choices":["Urine culture and sensitivity","A twenty-four hour urine collection","Serum creatinine alone","A repeat dipstick with an automated reader"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it measures the loss far more accurately than an estimate. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which two areas of point-of-care testing are noted as rapidly increasing?","choices":["Cholesterol and hemoglobin A1c testing","Pregnancy and ovulation testing","Rapid strep testing and rapid influenza antigen testing","Fentanyl testing and human immunodeficiency virus testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both are flagged as growing rapidly. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which density is described as the most dense naturally occurring material?","choices":["Metal","Fluid","Fat","Calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it absorbs most x-rays, though metal absorbs essentially all of them. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the decubitus position used to evaluate?","choices":["Bowel obstruction, because gravity separates the air from the fluid within the loops","Aortic calcification, because gravity displaces the overlying bowel gas out of the field","Pleural effusion, because gravity lets the fluid around the lung level out and be seen","Free air under the diaphragm, because gravity lets the air rise to the highest point"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fluid in the pleural space moves when the patient is laid on their side. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
